N('adas-israel-synagogue-dc','Adas Israel Synagogue (Capital Jewish Museum)','Washington','dc','worship','standing',38.8970,-77.0140,'site','open','drive','','1876',
  'Adas Israel was built in 1876 and is the oldest surviving synagogue in the District. The building has been moved three times; its last move, in 2019, brought it to 3rd and F Streets NW, where it is now part of the Capital Jewish Museum, which opened in 2023. The museum calls the synagogue its "centerpiece" and its largest object.',
  'The 1876 sanctuary, restored to its original appearance (the museum\'s words, as Wikipedia gives them), inside a new four-story museum building. Visitors reach it through the museum. What original fabric survives was not checked.',
  'A synagogue of 1876 that was moved three times and now sits inside a museum.',
  accn='Owned and run by the Capital Jewish Museum, 575 3rd Street NW (enter on F Street by the ramp). General admission is free with a suggested donation; a special exhibition is ticketed. Public paid garage at 3rd and G Streets NW; Judiciary Square Metro station is near. Hours from the museum\'s visit page, read November 2026; holiday hours differ.',
  links=[W('Lillian & Albert Small Capital Jewish Museum'),['Capital Jewish Museum: plan your visit','https://www.capitaljewishmuseum.org/visit']],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours='Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Monday and Tuesday.'),
N('georgetown-car-barn','Georgetown Car Barn (Capital Traction Company Union Station)','Washington','dc','rail','standing',38.90528,-77.07000,'site','view','drive','','1895–1897',
  'The Capital Traction Company built this Romanesque Revival terminal from 1895 to 1897 to Waddy Butler Wood\'s design, as a union station for several streetcar lines and a store for cars. It was also the terminal of the city\'s only cable car line. The company turned to electric power almost as soon as it opened, and a rebuild in 1911 changed the front and gutted the inside. Streetcars used it until 1950; it was then offices, and Georgetown University now owns it as an academic building. It was added to the National Register in 2019.',
  'A large three-story building with a clock tower, still shaped like a car barn. The 1911 rebuild altered the Romanesque front and left little of the 1897 interior, so look at the outside. The "Exorcist steps" beside it were built with it in 1895 to 1897 to join M Street and Prospect Street.',
  'A streetcar and cable-car terminal now used for lectures.',
  accn='Owned by Georgetown University, in use as a university building: look from Prospect Street and the steps. Nothing was read about whether visitors may go in, so treat the inside as closed. Parking was not checked.',
  links=[W('Georgetown Car Barn')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('dumbarton-house-dc','Dumbarton House','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.91083,-77.05611,'exact','open','drive','','1798–1799',
  'Samuel Jackson, a Philadelphia merchant, built this Federal-style house in 1798 and 1799, just before the federal government moved to the District. Its first long-term occupant was Joseph Nourse, the first Register of the Treasury. The National Society of The Colonial Dames of America bought it in 1928 for its headquarters, named it Dumbarton House, and opened its two main floors as a house museum in 1932. The house and nearby Dumbarton Oaks take their names from an 18th-century estate named for the Scottish "Rock of Dumbarton."',
  'A house museum set in about an acre of gardens, with period furniture, silver and ceramics. An early description quoted by Wikipedia calls it a two-story brick house with a wide central passage and two brick wings joined to it by covered ways. Wikipedia says guided and self-guided tours are offered.',
  'A Federal house museum on a Georgetown hill, built the year before the capital arrived.',
  accn='Owned by the National Society of The Colonial Dames of America, 2715 Q Street NW. The museum\'s own page would not open in this session, so hours, fees and parking were not checked: look up the current tour times with the museum before going.',
  links=[W('Dumbarton House')],conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('vigilant-firehouse-georgetown','Vigilant Firehouse, Georgetown','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.90453,-77.06297,'site','view','drive','','1844',
  'The Vigilant Fire Company, a private company, formed in 1817. It built this two-story firehouse in 1844, and it is the oldest firehouse still standing in the District. Engine Company 5 used it as a paid company from 1867, until it moved around the corner in 1883. After that the building was a bottling works, then a mattress factory until about 1970. A 2008 photograph shows it in use as a restaurant.',
  'A small gabled firehouse with a square wooden cupola. A stone in the front gable reads "Vigilant, Instituted, 1817", and under it is a wrought-iron "V" that also ties the walls together. A stone slab at ground level remembers "Bush, the Old Fire Dog", who died in 1869. The C&O Canal\'s commemorative obelisk stands just to the south.',
  'The oldest firehouse in the capital, with a memorial to a fire dog.',
  accn='On Wisconsin Avenue just north of the C&O Canal, in Georgetown; look from the sidewalk. Its present use and any hours were not checked (it was a restaurant in 2008).',
  links=[W('Vigilant Firehouse')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('georgetown-university-observatory','Georgetown University Astronomical Observatory (Heyden Observatory)','Washington','dc','inst','standing',38.90833,-77.07722,'area','view','walk','','1841–1844',
  'Father James Curley founded the observatory in 1841, chose its site on the college grounds and saw it built by 1844. In 1846 it was used to fix the latitude and longitude of Washington. Johann Hagen, director from 1888, installed a 12-inch telescope that Wikipedia says is still in continuous use. The astronomy department closed in 1972, and the Georgetown University Astronomical Society now runs the building.',
  'A small observatory on Observatory Hill, with the Heyden Memorial Garden on its east and north sides. The pin is from Wikipedia and was not checked against a campus map, so it is only a general position on the campus.',
  'A Jesuit observatory of 1844 that still has a working 12-inch telescope.',
  accn='On the private campus of Georgetown University. Whether outsiders may enter, and any public viewing nights, were not read: view it from the campus walks if the campus is open to you, and ask the university or its astronomical society before going close.',
  links=[W('Georgetown University Astronomical Observatory')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('latrobe-gate-navy-yard','Latrobe Gate, Washington Navy Yard','Washington','dc','fort','standing',38.87639,-76.99528,'exact','view','drive','','1806 and 1881',
  'Benjamin Henry Latrobe designed the main gate of the Washington Navy Yard, and it was built from 1805 to 1806. It is one of the oldest Greek Revival works in the country, and critics at the time thought it daring. In 1881 a brick Italianate Marine barracks was built around and over the gate. It is the oldest continuously manned Marine guardhouse in the United States.',
  'A gate arch at 8th and M Streets SE, now framed by the brick guard building of 1881: two stories above the gate and three at each side. It is a working guard post of the Navy, so look at it from the public sidewalk.',
  'Latrobe\'s Greek Revival gate with a Marine guardhouse built around it.',
  accn='A U.S. Navy installation: the gate is guarded and entry needs official clearance. Look from the sidewalk outside the fence. The Navy\'s visitor page would not open (HTTP 403), so rules for visitors are not checked.',
  links=[W('Latrobe Gate')],conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('main-pumping-station-dc','D.C. Water Main Pumping Station','Washington','dc','water','standing',38.87389,-77.00333,'exact','view','drive','','1904–1907',
  'A two-and-a-half-story Beaux Arts and Renaissance Revival building at 125 O Street SE, on the Anacostia between the Navy Yard and Nationals Park. Local architect Clement Didden designed it, and it was built from 1904 to 1907 on a steel and concrete frame. It still lifts the city\'s sewage and is staffed all year by DC Water. It was added to the National Register in 2012.',
  'The outside is reddish-brown brick with stone trim, in a City Beautiful style meant to make a utility look like a civic building. It is a working plant, so only the outside can be seen.',
  'A sewage pumping station dressed as a palace.',
  accn='Owned and operated by DC Water, an active utility with no public entry: look from O Street SE. Parking was not checked.',
  links=[W('D.C. Water Main Pumping Station')],brick='yes'),
N('boulder-bridge-rock-creek','Boulder Bridge, Rock Creek Park','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.94833,-77.04444,'site','public','walk','','1902',
  'A reinforced concrete arch carrying Beach Drive over Rock Creek, designed by W. J. Douglas and built in 1902 for $17,636. It was added to the National Register in 1980 together with the nearby Ross Drive Bridge, and both count in the Rock Creek Park Historic District.',
  'A single arch faced to look like a rustic stone bridge. Wikipedia describes the structure as reinforced concrete; what the boulder facing is made of was not checked.',
  'A concrete bridge dressed to look like natural boulders.',
  accn='In Rock Creek Park (National Park Service), where Beach Drive crosses Rock Creek; no hours or rules were read. See it from the road or the creek-side trail; parking was not checked.',
  links=[W('Boulder Bridge')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('thomas-law-house-dc','Thomas Law House (Honeymoon House)','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.87481,-77.01972,'site','view','drive','','1794–1796',
  'A syndicate headed by the land speculator James Greenleaf built this house between 1794 and 1796 near 6th and N Streets SW. Thomas Law moved in in March 1796 after marrying Eliza Parke Custis, Martha Washington\'s eldest granddaughter, so it got the name Honeymoon House; they left by that summer. It was the Mt. Vernon Hotel during the Civil War, then a hospital mission from about 1913, and Dr. Henry Hadley kept a clinic in it from 1923 to 1961. The National Register listed it in 1973.',
  'It dates from the 1790s. What shows on the outside and its present use were not checked.',
  'The honeymoon house of Martha Washington\'s granddaughter, one of the capital\'s earliest.',
  accn='Wikipedia\'s external links name the Tiber Island Cooperative Homes as owner (a page archived in 2007), so it is probably private: view it from the street and do not enter. Current ownership and use were not checked.',
  links=[W('Thomas Law House')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('franklin-school-planet-word','Franklin School (Planet Word)','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.90222,-77.02955,'site','open','drive','','1869',
  'Adolf Cluss designed this flagship school of 1869 in the German round-arch style, with a bust of Benjamin Franklin on the front. It was the first of eight modern public school buildings put up in the District from 1862 to 1875. A small plaque marks Alexander Graham Bell\'s photophone message of 1880, sent here by a beam of light. The building later became a homeless shelter, and in 2020 it became the Planet Word museum, after its inside was largely gutted.',
  'A large round-arched school building on Franklin Square. Its Great Hall, built to seat 1,000, has interior landmark protection, though Wikipedia says the interior was largely gutted for the museum. The photophone plaque is on the outside.',
  'A Reconstruction-era flagship school that is now a language museum.',
  accn='Planet Word, 925 13th Street NW, on Franklin Square. The museum\'s own page would not open in this session, so its hours, any fee and parking were not checked: look them up before going.',
  links=[W('Franklin School (Washington, D.C.)')],conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('glenwood-cemetery-chapel-dc','Glenwood Cemetery Mortuary Chapel','Washington','dc','worship','standing',38.92194,-77.00611,'exact','view','drive','','1892',
  'Glenn Brown, a local architect, designed this Richardsonian Romanesque chapel for Glenwood Cemetery, which commissioned it in August 1892. It was listed on the National Register in 1988 or 1989 (Wikipedia gives both). As of 1988, neither the cemetery nor the chapel had changed much since 1892. The cemetery, at 2219 Lincoln Road NE, is private and secular, and known for its elaborate Victorian and Art Nouveau monuments.',
  'A small Romanesque chapel among the graves. Whether the chapel is opened to visitors, and what it holds inside, was not checked.',
  'A cemetery chapel of 1892 in the Richardsonian Romanesque style.',
  accn='Glenwood Cemetery, Inc. is a private cemetery at 2219 Lincoln Road NE. Its page would not open on visiting hours or tours, so call or check the cemetery\'s site before going.',
  links=[W('Glenwood Cemetery Mortuary Chapel'),W('Glenwood Cemetery (Washington, D.C.)')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('conduit-road-schoolhouse-dc','Conduit Road Schoolhouse','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.92139,-77.09972,'exact','view','drive','','1864–1874',
  'A one-room schoolhouse at 4954 MacArthur Boulevard NW in the Palisades. It replaced a school of 1864 that burned, and Wikipedia files the present building under 1874. It closed in 1928, then served as a branch of the public library, and in 1965 it was saved to be a children\'s museum. It is on the National Register.',
  'A small one-room school beside the road. Wikipedia does not say what the building is used for now.',
  'A one-room school of the 1870s on the old canal road.',
  accn='Wikipedia gives no owner, hours or use for the building today. Look from MacArthur Boulevard, and check before expecting it to be open.',
  links=[W('Conduit Road Schoolhouse')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
